      "mechanism_card": {
        "hypothesis": "A dot's 100 m position is uncertain by about the kernel radius, so moving it up to 2 px onto the local maximum of the horizontal magnetic gradient recovers true-trace credit the kernel currently only half-credits.",
        "mechanism": "Faults and their damage zones truncate or offset magnetised units, so the horizontal gradient of TMI peaks along the structure. The grader gives a triangular credit max(1-d/3px,0), so a dot displaced 2 px from truth keeps only 0.33 of its unit credit; the same dot re-centred earns ~1.0.",
        "confounder": "Magnetic contacts with no Quaternary slip (intrusive contacts, remanence boundaries, survey seams between flight-line blocks) produce the same gradient peak; so do shallow alluvial magnetic contrasts.",
        "predicted": "MODEL: live change in [-0.005, +0.015], central +0.004. Below the 0.01 evidence floor this is a mechanism probe, not a score play.",
        "falsifier": "Live change <= -0.02 kills the positional-correction reading (the dots were already on true traces and the gradient peak is a mimicking contact); |change| < 0.01 is no evidence in either direction."
      },
